/// SBR wave 1, lane `bytes`, 2026-09-28: 15 rows over four small, stateless
/// or small-state helpers the dev census found native-won under `--jdk-only`
/// (`/data/sbr/nw-base-byclass.txt`: 3 each). Every row is a `Bridge` over a
/// method with `Code` on the JDK 21 and 25 images (declared, or for the five
/// `StringBuilder` rows inherited from `AbstractStringBuilder`); JDK 17 has
/// neither `StringBuilder.repeat` nor `Buffer$2`, so nothing there can call
/// those.
///
/// # What each group was, and why real bytecode is the better producer
///
/// * **`java/lang/StringBuilder` — the five rows
///   [`RETIRED_SHADOW_STRINGBUILDER_TRIPLES`] excluded.** Its doc comment left
///   `append(AbstractStringBuilder)`, `appendNull`, `getCoder`, `getValue` and
///   `repeat(CI)` out as "a triple with no receiver of its own", expecting
///   dispatch to reach `AbstractStringBuilder`'s retired copies. It does not:
///   a call on a `StringBuilder` receiver asks the receiver-keyed triple first,
///   so these five kept winning over the inherited bytecode (census: three of
///   them native-won in the dev suite). Same bodies, same real fields as the
///   118 rows already retired, so the reason for leaving them no longer holds.
/// * **`java/util/AbstractMap$SimpleEntry` — `getKey`, `getValue`, `hashCode`,
///   `equals`, `toString`.** Every producer of a `SimpleEntry` puts the key and
///   value in slots 0 and 1, which ARE the real `key`/`value` fields, so the
///   real bodies read what the natives read.
/// * **`java/nio/Bits$1.getName`** — two instructions returning `"direct"`;
///   `native_override.rs`' `is_direct_buffer_pool_counter_override` already
///   said the JDK's answer is right and forced only the three counters.
/// * **`java/nio/Buffer$2`: `getBufferBase`, both `newDirectByteBuffer`s,
///   `scaleShifts`.** Each real body is one call: `buffer.base()`,
///   `new DirectByteBuffer(addr, cap[, att, seg])`, `buffer.scaleShifts()`.
///   The natives re-derived the same answers (a by-name `hb` read, a class-name
///   token scan) and `scaleShifts(null)` answered 0 where the JDK NPEs.
///
/// # `jdk/internal/util/ByteArray` is NOT here — it became a reviewed `Intrinsic`
///
/// The lane's first cut retired all 20 `ByteArray` rows: the `Bridge` bodies
/// gave different answers from the JDK (no bounds check, no null check, raw NaN
/// bits from `setDouble`/`setFloat`). The real bodies were right but cost
/// 1.6-2.6x on `DataInputStream`/`DataOutputStream` (`SbrW1BytesBench`). By the
/// orchestrator's decision the natives were instead rewritten to the JDK's exact
/// semantics and registered `NativeKind::Intrinsic` (see the registration in
/// `native-builtins/src/lib.rs`). A table entry re-tags only an effective
/// `Bridge`, so listing them here would be inert; they are absent, and so is
/// the `jdk/internal/util/ByteArray` prefix.
///
/// # Left out, on purpose
///
/// * `AbstractMap$SimpleEntry.setValue`: the entry-set views in
///   `native-collections` (`alloc_live_entry` and its callers) mint a
///   `SimpleEntry` ONE SLOT WIDER than declared and keep the source map in
///   that undeclared slot 2; only the native `setValue` reads it and writes
///   through to the map. The real body would silently stop writing through —
///   the "reader that owned both ends of a convention" shape.
/// * `Bits$1.getCount` / `getMemoryUsed` / `getTotalCapacity`: the JDK's
///   counters are maintained by `Bits.reserveMemory`, which this VM never
///   reaches (its own allocator serves `allocateDirect`), so the real bodies
///   read zero forever. Measured: the enforcement dial on them turns
///   `RBufferPoolCount` red and the probe's `directCount>0` from `true` to
///   `false`.
/// * `Bits$1.getObjectName`, `Buffer$1.scaleShifts`,
///   `Buffer$2.acquireSession(Buffer)MemorySegment$Scope`: no image declares
///   them, so there is no bytecode to yield to.
/// * `Buffer$2.reserveMemory` / `unreserveMemory` / `hasSession` /
///   `isThreadConfined` / `pageSize`: the first two are the VM's own
///   direct-memory accounting (`RETIRED_SHADOW_L4_W19_TRIPLES`' note); the
///   session pair answers from the session shim the JOTP wave keeps; none of
///   the five was native-won in the census, so none was measured here.
///
/// # Measured
///
/// Azure linux, JDK 25.0.4+7, base `038a7f265` against this table plus the
/// `ByteArray` intrinsic (`6059eca13`):
///
/// ```text
///   regression suite SUITE=all --jdk-only   base 151/1   lane 151/1   per-vector identical (RJdkLogging)
///     native-won triples (census UNION)     730 -> 706
///   SbrW1Bytes vs HotSpot (63 lines)        base 20 differ   lane 0, --jdk-only and --nojit; drift 0
///   SbrW1BytesBench, base -> lane           DataInput 0.95-0.97x, DataOutput 0.80-0.85x (JIT),
///                                           0.94-0.97x --nojit; ObjectStream and UUID unchanged
/// ```
///
/// The record below has the per-arm detail.
///
/// docs/feature-designs/native-dispatch-architecture.md
static RETIRED_SHADOW_SBR_BYTES_TRIPLES: &[(&str, &str, &str)] = &[
    ("java/nio/Bits$1", "getName", "()Ljava/lang/String;"),
    (
        "java/nio/Buffer$2",
        "getBufferBase",
        "(Ljava/nio/Buffer;)Ljava/lang/Object;",
    ),
    (
        "java/nio/Buffer$2",
        "newDirectByteBuffer",
        "(JI)Ljava/nio/ByteBuffer;",
    ),
    (
        "java/nio/Buffer$2",
        "newDirectByteBuffer",
        "(JILjava/lang/Object;Ljava/lang/foreign/MemorySegment;)Ljava/nio/ByteBuffer;",
    ),
    ("java/nio/Buffer$2", "scaleShifts", "(Ljava/nio/Buffer;)I"),
    (
        "java/util/AbstractMap$SimpleEntry",
        "equals",
        "(Ljava/lang/Object;)Z",
    ),
    (
        "java/util/AbstractMap$SimpleEntry",
        "getKey",
        "()Ljava/lang/Object;",
    ),
    (
        "java/util/AbstractMap$SimpleEntry",
        "getValue",
        "()Ljava/lang/Object;",
    ),
    ("java/util/AbstractMap$SimpleEntry", "hashCode", "()I"),
    (
        "java/util/AbstractMap$SimpleEntry",
        "toString",
        "()Ljava/lang/String;",
    ),
];

/// SBR wave 2, lane NIOCH (2026-09-28): the socket / channel / selector
/// implementation classes. **One row retires; the family is HELD**, and the
/// size is the finding.
///
/// # The one row
///
/// `sun/nio/ch/SocketDispatcher.close(Ljava/io/FileDescriptor;)V`, native-won
/// in 3 vectors of the int1 census (every real `java.net.Socket` /
/// `ServerSocket` close goes `NioSocketImpl.close` -> `nd.close(fd)`). One
/// `Bridge` registration (`native-io/src/net.rs`, `register_sun_nio_ch_net`,
/// ambient `Bridge`). Its body was `close_net_fd_descriptor(fd, true)`. The
/// bytecode it shadows:
///
/// * 21 / 25: not declared on `SocketDispatcher`; inherited
///   `UnixDispatcher.close(fd)` = `close0(fd)`, ACC_NATIVE, registered in the
///   same function to `net_close` -- which IS `close_net_fd_descriptor(fd,
///   true)`. Retiring the shadow therefore runs one bytecode frame and lands
///   on the identical close.
/// * 17: declared, `FileDispatcherImpl.close0(fd)`, bound to the FILE table's
///   `native_fd_dispatcher_close0` -- which would miss every `net_sockets()`
///   id (disjoint by construction, `next_net_fd`) and leak the OS socket while
///   reporting success. Fixed in the commit this table lands with: under
///   `--jdk-only` that native closes a descriptor naming a live socket id the
///   way `net_close` does (`net::close_if_net_descriptor`). `--compatible`
///   keeps the shadow, never reaches the branch, and is unchanged.
///
/// # Why the family is held (measured, `cvm-sbr-int1`, dial and probe)
///
/// Under this VM the natives ARE the object model, three ways over:
///
/// 1. **Minted without `<init>`, state in side tables.** `SocketChannel.open`,
///    `ServerSocketChannel.open`, the `SelectorProviderImpl` factories and
///    `Selector.open` allocate `sun.nio.ch.SocketChannelImpl` /
///    `ServerSocketChannelImpl` / `EPollSelectorImpl` (`alloc_channel_as_impl`)
///    and keep their state in the identity-keyed `chan_fields` table plus the
///    `tcp_registry()` of `TcpStream`/`TcpListener`s. The real fields (`fd`,
///    `fdVal`, `state`, `readLock`, `writeLock`, `acceptLock`, the selector's
///    `interruptLock`, ...) stay null/0. Dial on the impl classes: every probe
///    row that touches a channel NPEs on one of them.
/// 2. **The NIO fd space is not the OS fd space.** Yielding the factories too
///    (real `SocketChannelImpl.<init>`) gets `Net.socket0` ids from
///    `net_sockets()` starting at `0x4000_0000`. The real `EPollSelectorImpl`
///    hands those to `EPoll.ctl` -- which passes them to `epoll_ctl(2)`
///    verbatim -- and `EPoll.wait` is a stub that returns 0. A real selector
///    cannot see a real channel. Measured: the probe's first two channel rows
///    diverge and the third hangs.
/// 3. **UDP and the async group are not modelled below the dispatcher.**
///    `Net.socket0` ignores its `stream` flag (a datagram bind builds a
///    `TcpListener`); `DatagramChannelImpl.send0`/`receive0` and
///    `IOUtil.makePipe` are unregistered (`UnsatisfiedLinkError`, measured);
///    `java.net.DatagramSocket` / `MulticastSocket` constructors write fd-table
///    ids into raw slots instead of the real `delegate`; the
///    `SimpleAsynchronousFileChannelImpl` carrier appends a private slot map
///    (`afc_base`) with `fdObj`/`threads`/`closeLock` null, and
///    `FileLockImpl.release` exists to keep the real `release` off it.
///
/// Also held on their own: `NativeThread.current()J` (native-won in 8
/// vectors) is ACC_NATIVE on JDK 17 -- a `SyntheticStub` there is dropped and
/// becomes an `UnsatisfiedLinkError`; `NativeThread.signal(J)V` and
/// `NativeDispatcher.preClose` shadow bytecode whose callee `signal0` is not
/// registered at all.
///
/// Dial pre-screen on the whole family, `SUITE=all --jdk-only`: 152/0 ->
/// 143/9 (RChannelToString, RSocketChannelInterrupt, RSslEndpointIdentification,
/// RSocketFastIo, RMulticastSocketClose, RMulticastSocketLoopback, RJdkNio,
/// RJdkNet, RJdkAsyncChannel), identical with the factories yielded too.
/// `SocketDispatcher` alone: see the record.
/// `the_sbr_nioch_wave_is_one_dispatcher_row_and_holds_the_channel_family`
/// holds the rest. Record:
/// `docs/feature-designs/profile-guided-inlining.md`.
static RETIRED_SHADOW_SBR_NIOCH_TRIPLES: &[(&str, &str, &str)] = &[(
    "sun/nio/ch/SocketDispatcher",
    "close",
    "(Ljava/io/FileDescriptor;)V",
)];

/// SBR wave 3, lane REFLECT (2026-09-29): core reflection. 25 triples over
/// eight classes retire; `Field`'s value accessors are HELD on measured cost,
/// and the object model underneath (the copy door, annotations, `Proxy`) is
/// HELD, each part with a measured reason.
///
/// # What retires, and why the state under it is real
///
/// * **The four generic carriers** (`sun/reflect/generics/reflectiveObjects/
///   {ParameterizedType,TypeVariable,WildcardType,GenericArrayType}Impl`), 15
///   rows: every accessor, `toString` and the inherited `getTypeName`. Under
///   `--jdk-only` every instance is minted on the REAL class with its REAL
///   named fields -- through the JDK's own `make` factories
///   (`generics::real_parameterized_type`, the `GenericArrayTypeImpl` arm) or
///   through `typesig_to_real_type` / `real_wildcard_type`, which write
///   `actualTypeArguments`/`rawType`/`ownerType`, `upperBounds`/`lowerBounds`
///   by name (`class_manager` pins the JDK declaration order). The real
///   bodies are field reads plus the lazy `reifyBounds`, which the natives
///   were re-implementing by hand. Yielding FIXES one row HotSpot disagrees
///   with: `Outer<String>.Inner<Integer>.toString()` renders the owner's
///   arguments (`Outer<java.lang.String>$Inner<java.lang.Integer>`) where the
///   native printed `Outer$Inner<java.lang.Integer>`.
/// * **`Field.setAccessible`, `canAccess`, `getGenericType`**, 3 triples (4
///   registrations: `setAccessible` is registered twice). The real bodies are
///   `AccessibleObject.checkCanSetAccessible` / `verifyAccess` over real
///   fields and the generic-signature parser, and this wave makes the one
///   field they need that the mint did not write REAL, in
///   `lang_class::create_field_object`: `signature` (the JVMS §4.7.9
///   attribute, as `create_method_object` has carried a method's since WP2.1).
///   Without it the real `getGenericType()` erases `Map<String, List<T>>` to
///   `Map` -- exactly why lane L3 held it (`RJdkReflect` "generic field type"
///   under the dial). The same commit writes HotSpot's `trustedFinal`
///   (`final && (static || hidden || record)`, previously hard-coded `false`),
///   which the real accessor factory keys its READ-ONLY accessor on; the
///   accessors below are held, so today it is read only by the yielded
///   `MethodHandle`-based path, but it is the state they need when they go.
///   Every native reader of the accessible flag (`read_*_accessible`,
///   `accessible_override_is_set`) consults the JDK `override` field FIRST,
///   so the real `setAccessible0` is seen by the natives that stay.
/// * **`Method.canAccess`, `getGenericReturnType`, `getGenericParameterTypes`,
///   `getTypeParameters`; `Constructor.canAccess`, `setAccessible`.** The
///   `Method` mint already carries `signature`; the two `setAccessible`/
///   `canAccess` pairs are `AccessibleObject.checkCanSetAccessible` /
///   `verifyAccess` over real fields. Yielding restores HotSpot's
///   `InaccessibleObjectException` message (it names the member and the
///   module: "Unable to make java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles$Lookup(java.lang.Class)
///   accessible: ..." for "Unable to make member accessible: ...").
///   `getGenericParameterTypes` needs the carrier rows above in the SAME
///   table: the real reifier hands back a real `WildcardTypeImpl` whose
///   `upperBounds` are still `FieldTypeSignature` tree nodes, and only the
///   real lazy `getUpperBounds` reifies them (under the dial with the native
///   still bound warm, `? extends U` printed as
///   `sun.reflect.generics.tree.TypeVariableSignature@..`).
/// * **`ReflectionFactory.getExecutableSharedParameterTypes`.** The native
///   worked around a null `langReflectAccess` that cannot happen here: this
///   VM's `SharedSecrets.getJavaLangReflectAccess` always returns the real
///   `ReflectAccess`, whose body is bytecode (`ex.getSharedParameterTypes()`,
///   i.e. the populated `parameterTypes`).
///
/// # HELD, with the measurement that held each
///
/// * **`Field`'s value accessors** -- `get`/`set` and the sixteen typed
///   `getX`/`setX`, 18 triples -- are CORRECT when yielded (every typed row of
///   the probe, widening, narrowing, final, static and null-receiver, matches
///   HotSpot once `trustedFinal` is real; the null receiver's NPE even gains
///   HotSpot's helpful message) and are held on COST: on a trial binary that
///   retired them, the real `MethodHandle`-based field accessor measured
///   2.6-4.0x slower than the native with the JIT and 4.5-6.1x `--nojit`
///   (`getInt` 1.9-2.8 us -> 7.3-7.7 us). They go when the `MethodHandle`
///   field-accessor path is as cheap as the native. SBR wave 5 lane REFLACC
///   linked the field-handle `invokeExact` (`lang_invoke::mh_field_link_fast`:
///   `MethodHandle.invokeExact` on a field getter 4.8-5.9 us -> 1.9-2.2 us)
///   and still HELD them: retired, `getInt` is 1.9-2.5x the native with the
///   JIT and 4.2-5.1x `--nojit`, the rest being `Reflection.getCallerClass`'s
///   full stack capture and the VM's un-memoized signature-polymorphic
///   dispatch -- `docs/feature-designs/native-dispatch-architecture.md`.
/// * **`java/lang/reflect/Proxy`** (4 rows) and `InvocationHandler.
///   invokeDefault`: the VM generates its own `$ProxyN` classes, which the real
///   `ProxyBuilder`'s `reverseProxyCache` has never seen. Dial on `Proxy`:
///   `isProxyClass(proxyClass)` and `getProxyClass(..)` answer `false`,
///   `getInvocationHandler` throws "not a proxy instance", annotation
///   instances stop being proxies; suite `RArrayStoreInterfaces`, `RJdkStrict`,
///   `RJdkProxy`, `RJdkJmx` red (each re-run alone).
/// * **The copy door** -- `ReflectionFactory.copyField/copyMethod/
///   copyConstructor` (native-won in every vector: boot) and
///   `Field/Method/Constructor.copy`. They are IDENTITY: the VM hands out one
///   shared object per member, and its `Method`/`Constructor`/`Field` carry
///   CratonVM extra slots past the JDK layout (descriptor, parameter count,
///   the pre-`override` accessible flag, the `METH` trust marker) that the
///   real `copy()` -- `new Method(...)` through `<init>` -- would not
///   allocate. Retiring the copy door needs those slots gone first.
/// * **Annotations** -- `get(Declared)Annotation(s)(ByType)`,
///   `isAnnotationPresent`, `getParameterAnnotations`, `getDefaultValue`,
///   `getAnnotatedType`/`getAnnotatedParameterTypes` on all three, and the
///   seven annotation getters of `TypeVariableImpl` (`getAnnotatedBounds`
///   included). The mint sites write `annotations`/`parameterAnnotations`/
///   `annotationDefault` null on purpose, and even with bytes the real
///   `AnnotationParser` needs a `jdk.internal.reflect.ConstantPool`, which
///   this VM does not provide (`ReflectionFactory.getConstantPool` answers
///   null -- also held). Dial: every annotation row reads empty/null
///   (`RAnnotationProxyGate`, `RJdkReflect` red). The `TypeVariableImpl`
///   getters are `BAD -> BAD` on an annotated type parameter (HotSpot `[@Tag,
///   @TagU]`, native AND bytecode `[]`), so yielding them buys nothing.
/// * **`Reflection.ensureNativeAccess`**: the real body reads
///   `Module.enableNativeAccess`, which `--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED`
///   never sets here (the grant lives in `panama::NativeAccessPolicy`).
///   Measured on `RJdkJni`: with the flag, the yielded path still prints the
///   JDK's four-line restricted-method WARNING; HotSpot and the native print
///   none.
/// * `Method.invoke`, `Constructor.newInstance`, `*.trySetAccessible`,
///   `*.getRoot`, `*.getParameters`: not native-won in the census and not
///   measured here (L3's `BAD -> BAD` verdict on the first two stands).
///
/// # Measured
///
/// Azure linux, JDK 25.0.4+7, control `cvm-sbr-int2` (sbr-orch `35aa5e5fc`)
/// against this table (`cvm-sbr-reflect-2`):
///
/// ```text
///   regression suite SUITE=all --jdk-only   control 152/0   lane 152/0   per-vector identical
///     native-won triples (census UNION)     639 -> 627 (13 family rows gone, +1 WeakReference.refersTo)
///   SbrW3Reflect vs HotSpot (146 rows)      control 13 differ   lane 10 (3 fixed, 0 broken); drift 0,
///                                           --nojit identical; unretire == control; --compatible == control
///   strict corpus                           same 4 real divergences as control
///   SbrW3ReflectBench (ns/op, ranges)       pt accessors 1.1-1.5x, pt.toString 1.8-3.2x JIT / 4-8x --nojit
/// ```
///
/// The record below has the per-arm detail.
///
/// docs/feature-designs/native-dispatch-architecture.md
